#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""shoal — mint a durable locus token; resolve it onto a later tree.

A file:line is a locator. It dies when the file splits. A shoal token is the
fingerprint plus a *signed repo claim* so a ticket can store the address.

The flipped assumption (vs keel v0.6): origin is **signed remotes at mint
time + required witnesses**, not live `git remote` config. Remotes are
config; witnesses are object occupancy. A stranger who can type
`git remote add extra git@github.com:victim.git` is not the project.
A fetched SHA is not the project. A true `file:// --depth 1` clone of
the signed project still resolves. Missing origin fail-closes unless
--any-repo.

Kept from keel / pin v0.4–v0.5 (not re-attacked here):
  * leftover stubs at the old path are not identity
  * extract-and-keep is not a move
  * a missing --to ref is an error, not a deletion
  * a truncated token fails closed
"""
